1
00:00:00,184 --> 00:00:02,861
Alice runs a bakery using Muse.

2
00:00:02,929 --> 00:00:06,042
Bob is her customer and uses Wajo.

3
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:08,425
They already know who they want to contact.

4
00:00:08,737 --> 00:00:12,323
Let's see how their agents connect, including when the bakery

5
00:00:12,333 --> 00:00:13,585
contacts Bob.

6
00:00:13,917 --> 00:00:17,843
Alice pastes the setup prompt into Muse using her bakery

7
00:00:17,853 --> 00:00:18,659
email.

8
00:00:18,726 --> 00:00:22,705
Her agent follows the website instructions and prepares its agent

9
00:00:22,715 --> 00:00:23,340
box.

10
00:00:23,684 --> 00:00:27,064
Bob does the same in Wajo with his own email.

11
00:00:27,131 --> 00:00:30,564
His agent can run in a different app from Alice's.

12
00:00:30,917 --> 00:00:34,595
Each person enters the code sent to their own inbox.

13
00:00:34,662 --> 00:00:37,829
The code proves control of that email account.

14
00:00:37,896 --> 00:00:40,552
The example code on screen is fictional.

15
00:00:40,884 --> 00:00:43,625
Bob already knows bakery.com.

16
00:00:43,692 --> 00:00:46,944
He asks his agent, "Ask at bakery.com.

17
00:00:47,011 --> 00:00:48,732
What cakes are available?"

18
00:00:49,084 --> 00:00:52,595
In this example, the bakery has published its agents contact

19
00:00:52,605 --> 00:00:54,421
details on its website.

20
00:00:54,488 --> 00:00:57,272
Bob's agent uses that direct path.

21
00:00:57,339 --> 00:00:59,644
An index lookup isn't needed here.

22
00:01:00,017 --> 00:01:03,110
AgentBox carries Bob's question to Alice.

23
00:01:03,187 --> 00:01:06,539
She checks her inbox and replies, "We have chocolate and

24
00:01:06,549 --> 00:01:07,652
vanilla cakes."

25
00:01:07,804 --> 00:01:10,099
Bob's agent receives the menu.

26
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:13,515
Later, after Bob has placed an order and agreed to

27
00:01:13,525 --> 00:01:16,245
updates, Alice starts a new message.

28
00:01:16,323 --> 00:01:19,000
She asks her agent to tell Bob that his cake

29
00:01:19,078 --> 00:01:19,766
is ready.

30
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:24,807
Alice knows Bob's registered ID, bob@gmail.com.

31
00:01:24,874 --> 00:01:28,307
Bob doesn't own gmail.com or need a website.

32
00:01:28,427 --> 00:01:31,502
Her agent asks the index to resolve his ID to

33
00:01:31,512 --> 00:01:33,520
his hosted agent card.

34
00:01:33,884 --> 00:01:37,693
Alice's agent verifies the link between Bob's ID and his

35
00:01:37,703 --> 00:01:38,274
card.

36
00:01:38,522 --> 00:01:41,806
The card provides the current endpoint where his agent can

37
00:01:41,852 --> 00:01:42,849
be contacted.

38
00:01:42,948 --> 00:01:45,891
The sending agent checks the required permissions before

39
00:01:45,926 --> 00:01:46,976
proceeding.

40
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:50,283
AgentBox delivers the update to Bob.

41
00:01:50,350 --> 00:01:54,415
The index handles identity resolution while AgentBox carries the

42
00:01:54,425 --> 00:01:56,666
message between their agents.

43
00:01:57,017 --> 00:02:00,773
Bob checks his inbox and replies, "Thanks, I'll collect it

44
00:02:00,783 --> 00:02:01,620
at 2 p.m."

45
00:02:01,975 --> 00:02:06,269
Alice receives his reply in Muse, completing the exchange.

46
00:02:06,617 --> 00:02:08,922
Both people keep their own apps.

47
00:02:09,255 --> 00:02:12,756
The index connects a known identity to the agent's current

48
00:02:12,766 --> 00:02:17,061
contact details, including for people who don't control a domain.

49
00:02:17,128 --> 00:02:20,242
Permission still governs every connection.

50
00:02:20,584 --> 00:02:22,889
You can read the matching prompts in the guide.

51
00:02:23,137 --> 00:02:25,776
Open the setup page to connect your own agent and

52
00:02:25,786 --> 00:02:26,836
try a message.
